it would be an understatement to say  that Britain and the USA's relationship  got off to a rocky start in 1812 Britain  and America were at war but just over a  century later they were fighting  shoulder-to-shoulder in World War one  which obviously raises the question when  did Britain and America become allies  they had to be a point where both went  from actively disliking each other to  working alongside each other now before  continuing it's important to note that  even in the immediate aftermath of  independence America and Britain still  had strong economic ties this isn't to  say that things were always great  between the two and there were some  pretty major conflicts of interest in  the years after the war of 1812 finished  the first and perhaps the most obvious  was the Monroe Doctrine established in  1823 this was where the United States by  threat of military intervention declared  an end to any new European colonial  adventures in the Americas and for  Britain this was potentially a problem  because it had economic interest in  South America in reality though it  wasn't too much of a problem at all  because in 1833 it landed a force and  the Falkland Islands and America did  nothing in 1842 the u.s. did warn  Britain against interference in Hawaii  which Britain did heed because America's  international power was certainly  growing and it was this fact that  America was a rapidly growing great  power whose interest could no longer be  ignored which would dominate  anglo-american relations to the  following century so the next major  event which could have changed the  anglo-american dynamic was the American  civil war which broke out in 1861  Britain had already abolished slavery at  this point in so you would think that it  would have preferred the Union over the  Confederacy but you'd be wrong now  Britain had no great affection for the  Confederacy in its lay voting practices  its preferential treatment was purely  economic mostly due to cotton imports  Britain did consider formally  recognizing the Confederacy in  anglo-american relations were severely  harmed by what's called the Trent affair  this was where the US government seized  a ship with Confederate diplomats headed  for Britain which to Britain looked like  the USA telling it who it could and  could not speak to in the end cooler  heads prevailed and Britain didn't get  involved although it did construct a  very powerful warship the CSS Alabama  for the Confederacy and after the Civil  War was over Britain paid compensation  to the United States thus only fifty  years away from World War one Britain  and America still didn't really care  much for each other so what changed this  well relations actually started to  improve during Britain's border dispute  with Venezuela in 1895 this developed  into somewhat of a crisis and the u.s.  intervened and arbitrated siding mostly  with Britain for Britain America had  conducted itself in a well-mannered  fashion and given  Britta most of what it had wanted and  for America by accepting American  Arbitration Britain had yielded its  right to influence South America the  other reason for anglo-american reproach  meant was simple converging interests  America had emerged from its Civil War a  more centralized industrial power and  after its defeat of Spain in 1898 it  possessed a global Empire to and for  Britain things were rapidly changing as  well its empire and global position was  being threatened by Russia and the newly  formed Germany Britain had long  practiced a policy of isolationism with  respect to the European powers but since  they too were industrializing this had  the potential to threaten Britain's  position thus her most natural ally  seemed to be the United States since it  was half a world away and had its own  sphere of influence plus they spoke the  same language and shared many values it  was at the turn of the 20th century  under President Theodore Roosevelt the  dimeric around Britain really started to  become friends particularly with respect  to one foreign policy stance they didn't  like Germany this agreement allowed for  Britain to move its fleet away from its  Caribbean colonies back to Britain to  defend itself from Germany while  security in the Caribbean would fall to  the US after the first world war broke  out Germany's actions such as u-boat  attacks on American shipping in the  Zimmerman telegram combined with the  fact that if Britain and France lost the  war they wouldn't be able to pay their  American loans back assured US  intervention on their side and from that  point onwards both the US and UK were  largely in the same camps barring a  couple of mishaps here and there I hope  you enjoyed this episode and thank you  for watching with extra thanks to these  patrons you can see on screen for their  continued support and an extra special  thanks to James Bissonnette David  archaeologists as ARCA Flash Danny  Maloney party-boy Koch coolin Castleman  Rob Waterhouse Chris wicker Gustav Swan  David Silverman Winston Kate woods  Christian checky Maggie packs Kowski  Lexi Schwinn Anthony Beckett Robert  Wetzel Skye Chappell Ike spinning three  plates are on the white and Michael  Reynolds
 

